      "text": "To find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) when \\(x\\) is an odd number, we start by analyzing each factor separately and then combining the results.\n\nFirst, consider the expression modulo 2. Since \\(x\\) is an odd number, \\(x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\\) or \\(x \\equiv -1 \\pmod{2}\\). Therefore, we can substitute \\(x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{2}\\) into each term of the expression:\n\n1. \\(10x + 2 \\equiv 10 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\equiv 10 + 2 \\equiv 12 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2}\\)\n2. \\(10x + 6 \\equiv 10 \\cdot 1 + 6 \\equiv 10 + 6 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2}\\)\n3. \\(5x + 5 \\equiv 5 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\equiv 5 + 5 \\equiv 10 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{2}\\)\n\nSince each term in the expression evaluates to a multiple of 2, the entire expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) is a product of three even numbers and is thus divisible by 8. Now we need to check if the expression is always divisible by 4 when \\(x\\) is an odd number.\n\nNext, consider the expression modulo 4. Since \\(x\\) is an odd number, \\(x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{4}\\) or \\(x \\equiv -1 \\pmod{4}\\). However, since the expression modulo 4 does not depend on the specific value of \\(x\\) as long as it is odd, we can directly evaluate the expression for odd \\(x\\):\n\n1. \\(10x + 2 \\equiv 10 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\equiv 10 + 2 \\equiv 12 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}\\)\n2. \\(10x + 6 \\equiv 10 \\cdot 1 + 6 \\equiv 10 + 6 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{4}\\)\n3. \\(5x + 5 \\equiv 5 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\equiv 5 + 5 \\equiv 10 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{4}\\)\n\nSince one of the terms is a multiple of 4 and the other two terms are even, the entire expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) is always a product of three even numbers and is thus divisible by 4. Now we need to check if the expression is always divisible by 16 when \\(x\\) is an odd number.\n\nFinally, consider the expression modulo 8. Since \\(x\\) is an odd number, \\(x \\equiv 1 \\pmod{8}\\) or \\(x \\equiv -1 \\pmod{8}\\). We need to evaluate the expression for odd \\(x\\):\n\n1. \\(10x + 2 \\equiv 10 \\cdot 1 + 2 \\equiv 10 + 2 \\equiv 12 \\equiv 4 \\pmod{8}\\)\n2. \\(10x + 6 \\equiv 10 \\cdot 1 + 6 \\equiv 10 + 6 \\equiv 16 \\equiv 0 \\pmod{8}\\)\n3. \\(5x + 5 \\equiv 5 \\cdot 1 + 5 \\equiv 5 + 5 \\equiv 10 \\equiv 2 \\pmod{8}\\)\n\nSince one of the terms is a multiple of 8 and the other two terms are not, the entire expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) is always a product of three even numbers and is thus divisible by 16.\n\nTherefore, the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) when \\(x\\) is an odd number is \\(16\\).\n\nThe final answer is \\(\\boxed{16}\\).",
