    {
      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve the problem, we need to find the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) for any odd number \\(x\\).\n\n1. **Factorize the expression**:\n   \\[\n   (10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5) = 2(5x+1) \\cdot 2(5x+3) \\cdot (x+1) = 4(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Analyze the factors**:\n   - Among the factors \\(5x+1, 5x+3,\\) and \\(x+1\\), one of them must be divisible by 3. If \\(x\\) is odd, then \\(x+1\\) is even, so \\(5x+1\\) is even. Therefore, \\(5x+1\\) is divisible by 2.\n   - Among \\(5x+1, 5x+3,\\) and \\(x+1\\), one of them must be divisible by 5. Since \\(x\\) is odd, \\(x+1\\) is even, so \\(5x+1\\) modulo 5 is 1, and \\(5x+3\\) modulo 5 is 3. Therefore, \\(5x+3\\) is divisible by 5.\n\n3. **Determine the greatest common divisor**:\n   - The product \\(4(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\\) is divisible by \\(2 \\cdot 5 = 10\\).\n   - Since one of the factors \\(5x+3\\) is divisible by 3, the expression is divisible by 9.\n   - The product \\(4(5x+1)(5x+3)(x+1)\\) is also divisible by 150 (since it is divisible by 2 \\(\\times\\) 5 \\(\\times\\) 3 \\(\\times\\) 5).\n\nTherefore, the largest integer that always divides the expression \\((10x+2)(10x+6)(5x+5)\\) for any odd number \\(x\\) is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{900}\n\\]",
